+**JavaScript option and universe-flag derivation.** `allowJs` and `checkJs`
+in the retained context are effective, materialized booleans from the selected
+compiler's resolved configuration, including the retained configuration graph.
+For the selected option projection, every node of that graph whose `kind` is
+`jsconfig` (the exact basename law of `TypeScriptConfigGraphV1`) supplies
+`allowJs=true` as if written in that node, unless the node itself writes
+`allowJs`. Inheritance then applies over the graph: a node's own options,
+including that supplied value, take precedence over the bases it extends, and
+later `extends` entries over earlier ones. After inheritance an `allowJs` value
+wins, including `false`; otherwise `allowJs` derives from the effective
+`checkJs`, whose omitted default is `false`. So a `jsconfig.json` entry extending
+a base that writes `allowJs: false` is effective `allowJs=true` unless the entry
+writes `allowJs` itself, and a `tsconfig.json` entry extending a base named
+`jsconfig.json` inherits `true`. The pre-Plan marker observation
+named `allowJs` also means this effective value, never mere presence of the raw
+configuration property. `js-allowjs` versus `ts-tsconfig` selection uses that
+value. The synthesized profile keeps its separately specified `true`/`false`
+values. This derivation agrees with the version-pinned [TypeScript compiler
+computed option](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/v5.6.3/src/compiler/utilities.ts#L8919);
+that source is supporting evidence, not a required product toolchain version.
+
+The two universe flags have exact, distinct laws:
+- `jsAdmittedToProgram = configProjection.honoredOptions.allowJs AND
+  (len(universe.jsRootFiles) > 0)`.
+- `jsDiagnosticsEnabled = configProjection.honoredOptions.checkJs`.
+
+`jsRootFiles` is the admitted compiler root-file set already joined to the
+universe; the first flag is about those JS roots, not a claim that every possible
+transitive JavaScript module was found. Enabling `allowJs` with no JS roots does
+not make the first flag true. Diagnostic enablement does not require a nonempty
+JS-root set and does not attest that any diagnostic ran or that compilation
+succeeded. An explicit `allowJs=false` with `checkJs=true` retains those effective
+option values; they do not assert that the compiler accepts that configuration.
+The option projection must not rewrite explicit `false` to `true` to suppress
+a configuration diagnostic. **This law introduces no compiler-option diagnostic
+refusal.** A compiler option diagnostic, including TypeScript diagnostic 5052 for
+`allowJs=false` with `checkJs=true`, neither refuses admission nor creates a
+deficiency, native cause or diagnostic carrier under this law, and this contract
+names no owner that routes such a diagnostic: a universe with those effective
+values is admitted with them, and whether the compiler accepted the configuration
+is not recorded by these flags (a stated limit, not a delegation). The law changes
+no effective option value, typed-context field or schema admission beyond the
+derivation above.
+The corresponding native context/universe field disagreement refuses through
+`native.universe-context-field-mismatch:<field>`.
+
+These options and flags are universe keys. `checkJs` controls diagnostics, and
